[s0l0!-header][h1]Comments[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

[b]Comments[/b] are explanatory statements that you can include in the C++ code to explain what the code is doing. 
The compiler ignores everything that appears in the comment, so none of that information shows in the result. 

A comment beginning with [b]two slashes (//) [/b]is called a single-line comment. The slashes tell the compiler to ignore everything that follows, until the end of the line. 

[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="cpp" codeId="176"]#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
   [b]// prints "Hello world"[/b]
   cout << "Hello world!";
   return 0;
}[/code]
When the above code is compiled, it will ignore the [b]// prints "Hello world" [/b]statement[b].[/b]
[note]Adding comments to your code is a good practice. It facilitates a clear understanding of the code for you and for others who read it.[/note]
